What are linked lists:

a linked list is a sequence of elements where each element (node) holds its data plus a pointer to the next node. Unlike an array, the elements aren't sitting next to each other in memroy - they're scattered about, only connected by pointers.

    [data, pointer] -> [data, pointer] -> [data, pointer] -> [data, pointer]

1. linked lists do not have fixed size

2. the insertion and deletion is fast O(1), we just repoint pointers, whereas in an array, we must shift the elements.

3. random access array[5] is O(1) in arrays, whereas in a pointer, it is O(n), we must walk from the start. This is the tradeoff

so you can make a struct for every node in a linked list and make it very node point to the other one.

Linked lists can be:
- Singly linked (like the diagram above)
- Double linked (so that you can also go backwards in the list)
- Circular (so that the last node points back to the first instead of to null.





